  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tricholomopsis formosa is a striking woodland mushroom characterized by its vibrant coloration and distinctive gill attachment. This fungus belongs to the Phyllotopsidaceae family and is a notable species found in the forest ecosystems of New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Bright Coloration",
      "description": "Features vivid orange to red-orange caps with yellow or yellowish gill edges, making it easily recognizable in the field",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill Attachment",
      "description": "Gills are notably decurrent (running down the stem) and display a distinctive yellow color that contrasts with the cap",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "The stem is typically yellow or yellowish, often with a more solid construction and lacking a ring or cup at the base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tricholomopsis formosa grows in coniferous and mixed forests of New England, typically fruiting on or near decaying wood and forest debris during late summer and fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The vibrant orange and yellow coloration of Tricholomopsis formosa may serve as a warning to potential predators, as some Tricholomopsis species contain compounds that can cause gastrointestinal distress if consumed.",
